MIOpen (pinned tag rocm-6.4.4, commit 4d17e9a): add ConvHipWinogradF2x3, a
Winograd F(2x2,3x3) solver for 3x3, stride-1, pad-1 fp32 and fp16 convolutions
on gfx8, forward and backward data.

WHY
winograd-bin-gfx803-disable.patch takes MIOpen's binary Winograd solvers off
gfx803 because they corrupt memory and hang the GPU there. That leaves the
GEMM solvers (GemmFwdRest, GemmBwdRest) for every 3x3 convolution: an im2col
copy that expands the input 9x, then one large GEMM. Every other Winograd
solver in this MIOpen is unusable on gfx803: ConvMPBidirectWinograd and the
multi-pass weight-gradient solvers run gfx9 assembly transform kernels and
reject gfx8 in IsApplicable, and the rest are gfx9+ or fp16-dot only.

Winograd F(2x2,3x3) needs 2.25x fewer multiplies than direct or im2col
convolution and keeps the GEMM it reduces to large. This solver splits it into
three small HIP kernels around one strided-batched rocBLAS GEMM:
  filter transform  w -> U[16][K][C]  (for backward data, the 180-degree
                    rotated filter with the channel axes swapped)
  input transform   x -> V[16][C][P]  (P = N * ceil(H/2) * ceil(W/2) tiles)
  GEMM              M[t] = U[t] * V[t] for the 16 tile positions t
  output transform  M -> y
The kernels are plain one-thread-per-(channel, tile) HIP code compiled through
MIOpen's normal HIP kernel path, with no persistent launch and no LDS, unlike
the binary kernels above. On gfx803 with this repo's SGEMM shim the GEMM runs
on the shim, so no Tensile fp32 kernel is involved. The solver is registered
in the Winograd list, so Find benchmarks it against the GEMM solvers and keeps
whichever is faster per shape.

For fp16 tensors the workspace is fp16 and the transforms still compute in
fp32, so values round only where they are stored; the GEMM goes through
MIOpen's half-precision rocBLAS call, which accumulates in fp32. Without this
solver gfx803 has no fast fp16 3x3 convolution at all: the binary Winograd
solvers are off, and the GEMM path's backward data hits a rocBLAS fp16 layout
that is 15-25x slower than the others (see the SGEMM shim's header).

Measured on an RX 590 (gfx803), batch 1, SD1.5 UNet shapes, GEMM solvers
against this solver as Find selected it:
  320->320  at 64x64: fwd 2.99 -> 1.50 ms, bwd-data 3.01 -> 1.55 ms
  1280->640 at 32x32: fwd 6.36 -> 2.97 ms, bwd-data 5.57 -> 3.46 ms
  2560->1280 at 16x16: fwd 7.65 -> 5.33 ms; bwd-data stays on GEMM (5.38)
  1280->1280 at 8x8 and the 4-channel input conv stay on GEMM.
Results are more accurate than the GEMM path's: relative error against a
float64 CPU reference is 7e-7 to 2.4e-6, against 2e-6 to 7e-6.

fp16, the same 17 shapes (batch 1 and 2), summed over all of them: forward
59.3 -> 36.7 ms and backward data 388.2 -> 32.0 ms against the GEMM path, close
to fp32's 34.7 and 33.8 ms on this solver; relative error against a float64
CPU reference of the same fp16 inputs 6e-4 to 1.1e-3, against the GEMM path's
3e-4 to 6e-4, since the transformed tiles are stored in fp16. The SD1.5 UNet
forward at a 64x64 latent then takes 394 ms in fp16 against 390 ms in fp32
(452 ms in fp16 without this solver). SD1.5 LoRA
training (fp32, batch 1, gradient checkpointing) runs at 4.05-4.3 s/step against
4.75 with the GEMM solvers, same seed and loss. The first steps after the
find-db is cleared take 30-50 s each while Find benchmarks each new bucket
size; later runs reuse the records. The SD1.5 VAE encode that
exposed the binary kernels' corruption runs 14 of its 28 conv problems on this
solver and produces latents identical to a CPU encode in 3 of 3 runs.

To reproduce the comparison: run torch.nn.functional.conv2d and
torch.nn.grad.conv2d_input for a 3x3 pad-1 conv on gfx803 with
MIOPEN_DEBUG_CONV_HIP_WINOGRAD_F2X3=0 and without, each with an empty
MIOPEN_USER_DB_PATH, and compare times and results against a float64 CPU
run. MIOPEN_DEBUG_FIND_ONLY_SOLVER=ConvHipWinogradF2x3 forces this solver.

A user find-db written before this solver existed keeps returning its old
choice for every convolution it has a record for, so delete the user
*.ufdb.txt files once after installing a build with this patch.

